3.8.2.m:
There are four kinds of water;
four-hoofed is the horse belonging to Prajāpati, for success.
They are collected from the quarters;
water is in the quarters;
water is food;
food is born from water;
whatever food is born from water,
that he secures.
In them he cooks the boiled rice mess for the priests;
thereby he deposits semen.

It consists of four dishes;
he is firmly established in the quarters.
There are gold plates on both sides;
he bestows brilliance upon him on both sides.
He lifts it off, for it to be well-cooked;
it has clarified butter, for sacrificial fitness.
Four men of ṛṣi descent eat of it;
he offers into the light of the quarters.
He gives four pieces of gold;
he secures the lights of the quarters.

3.8.5.m:
"Having sacrificed with this sacrificial horse,
may this king attain his full lifespan!"
Lifespan indeed is the Udgātṛ;
lifespan are the chamberlains and charioteers.
With lifespan he thus places lifespan in him.
They are a hundred each.
A man has a lifespan of a hundred [years] and a hundred powers;
he thus firmly establishes [him] in lifespan and in power. There are four hundred [in all];
four are the quarters;
he firmly establishes [him] in the quarters.

3.8.6.m:
Just as indeed [part] of an oblation that has been scooped out spills,
so indeed of this horse [a part] spills,
when, having been washed, they let [him] go unseized.
When he recites the drops-verses,
he renders him entirely offered, for the sake of non-spilling;
for that is unspilled,
whatever spills of what is offered.
He recites a thousand [verses];
commensurate with a thousand is the heavenly realm,
for the winning of the heavenly realm.

If he should recite a measured number,
he would win what is measured.
He recites an unmeasured number;
unmeasured is the heavenly realm,
for the attainment of the heavenly realm.
He offers the drops-oblations;
whatever waters are of rain,
those he secures.
He offers on this [earth];
this [earth] indeed is the fire Vaiśvānara.

He thus establishes them firmly upon this [earth].
Prajāpati said:
"In the drops-oblations indeed I conclude the Horse Sacrifice;
having concluded it through that, I thence go about."
He says: "To Agni, svāhā!";
he offers him to Agni.
He says: "To Soma, svāhā!";
he offers him to Soma.
He says: "To Savitṛ, svāhā!";
he offers him to Savitṛ.

He says: "To Sarasvatī, svāhā!";
he offers him to Sarasvatī.
He says: "To Pūṣan, svāhā!";
he offers him to Pūṣan.
He says: "To Bṛhaspati, svāhā!";
he offers him to Bṛhaspati.
He says: "To the delight of the waters, svāhā!";
he offers him to the waters.
He says: "To Vāyu, svāhā!";
he offers him to Vāyu.

He says: "To Mitra, svāhā!";
he offers him to Mitra.
He says: "To Varuṇa, svāhā!";
he offers him to Varuṇa.
He offers him to these deities.
He offers them to the completion of tens.
The Virāj has ten syllables;
food is the Virāj;
through the Virāj he secures food.
He falls away from this world
who offers oblations away [from himself];
he offers turning back again and again;
he firmly establishes himself in this world.
He indeed proclaimed this completion of the Horse Sacrifice for the sake of non-spilling;
for that is unspilled,
whatever spills of a sacrifice that has been completed.

3.8.7.m:
"Sprinkled acceptable to Prajāpati [are you]," standing on the east facing west, he sprinkles [him];
Prajāpati is the eater of food, full of heroic power among the gods;
he thus places food and heroic power in him;
therefore the horse is the greatest eater of food and has the greatest heroic power among domestic animals.
"To Indra and Agni [are you]," [he sprinkles him] from the south;
Indra and Agni are the most splendidly mighty and the strongest among the gods;
he thus places splendorous might and power in him;
therefore the horse is the most splendidly mighty and the strongest among domestic animals.
"To Vāyu [are you]," [he sprinkles him] from the west;
Vāyu is the swiftest, the most fleet-running among the gods;

he thus places speed in him;
therefore the horse
is the swiftest, the most fleet-running among domestic animals.
"To the Viśve Devas [are you]," [he sprinkles him] from the north;
the Viśve Devas are the most renowned among the gods;
he thus places renown in him;
therefore the horse is the most renowned among domestic animals.
"To the gods [are you]," [he sprinkles him] from below;
the gods are the most honored among the gods;
he thus places honor in him;
therefore the horse is the most honored among domestic animals.

"To all the gods [are you]," [he sprinkles him] from above;
all the gods are brilliant and furious;
he thus places brilliance and fury in him;
therefore the horse is brilliant and the most furious among domestic animals.
"To heaven [are you], to the intermediate space [are you], to earth [are you]," he says;
he sprinkles him for these worlds.
"To the existent [are you], to the non-existent [are you], to the waters [are you], to the plants [are you], to all beings [are you]," he says;
therefore all beings live upon one who sacrifices with the Horse Sacrifice.
The discussers of sacred lore say:
"Since the horse belongs to Prajāpati,
why then does he sprinkle him for other deities as well?"
In the horse indeed all deities are contained.
When he sprinkles him [saying], "To all beings [are you],"
he makes the deities inhere in him;
therefore all deities are contained in the horse.

3.8.8.m:
Just as indeed [part] of an oblation that has been scooped out spills,
so indeed of this horse [a part] spills,
when, having been sprinkled, they let [him] go unseized.
When he offers the horse's activities,
he renders him entirely offered, for the sake of non-spilling;
for that is unspilled,
whatever spills of what is offered.
He says: "To the sound 'īm', svāhā! To that which is accompanied by the sound 'īm', svāhā!";
these indeed are the horse's activities;
with these activities he makes him prosper.

On this point they say:
"The horse's activities are non-oblations;
these should not be offered."
And then, on the contrary, they say:
"They must indeed be offered;
here indeed, knowing thus, one concludes the Horse Sacrifice,
when he offers the horse's activities;
therefore they must be offered."
[Otherwise] he places him outside of his proper abode;
he generates a rival for him.

‘If he were to offer at each mouth of the sacrifice,
he would deprive the sacrificer of domestic animals;
he would fall down from the heavenly realm;
he would become worse.’
They should be offered only once.
He does not deprive the sacrificer of domestic animals;
he wins the heavenly realm;
he does not become worse.
He offers forty-eight horse-forms.
The jagatī metre consists of forty-eight syllables;
the horse belongs to the jagatī, [is] sacred to Prajāpati, for prosperity.
He offers one extra;
therefore, one among offspring is thriving.

3.8.9.m:
‘Extending by mother, prevailing by father,’ he says.
This [earth] indeed is the mother;
yonder [sky] is the father.
To these two he delivers him.
‘You are a horse, you are a courser,’ he says;
he thereby instructs him;
therefore well-instructed offspring are born.
‘You are a racer,’ he says;
therefore the horse surpasses all domestic animals;
therefore the horse attains preeminence among all domestic animals.

He attains fame [and] preeminence,
who knows thus.
‘You are a male, you are a steed, you are a runner, you are a courser,’ he says;
he thereby proclaims his form [and] his greatness.
‘Yayur is your name,’ he says;
that indeed is the horse’s dear name;
with his dear name he addresses him.
Therefore, even two enemies having met,
if they call [each other] by name,
become friends indeed.

‘Follow the flight of the Ādityas,’ he says;
he causes him to go to the Ādityas.
‘To Agni svāhā! Svāhā to Indrāgni!’ thus he offers the preliminary offerings;
he walks ahead past the hating rival.
‘You are being, to being thee, to what has been thee, to what will be thee!’ thus he releases [him], for wholeness.
‘O gods that guard the quarters, protect this horse consecrated for sacrifice for the gods,’ he says.
A hundred sons of kings on couches, indeed, are the gods that guard the quarters;
to them he delivers him.
Capable indeed is the released horse of going to the furthest distance.
‘Here is support, svāhā! Here is restraint, svāhā! Here is delight, svāhā! Here is revelry, svāhā!’—thus he offers at the four feet.

These indeed are the horse’s bond;
with them he binds him;
therefore the horse, though released, comes to the tether;
therefore the horse, though released, does not abandon the tether.
The horse sacrifice is indeed the realm;
they strive hard in the realm,
who guard the horse for the sacrifice.
Those among them who reach the conclusion,
they go from realm to realm;
but those who do not reach the conclusion,
they are cut off from the realm.
He is indeed poured away
who, being weak, sacrifices with the horse sacrifice.
If enemies should find the horse,
his sacrifice would be destroyed.
Four hundred [men] protect [it],
for the non-destruction of the sacrifice.
Then, bringing another, they should sprinkle [it];
that indeed is the expiation thereafter.

3.8.10.m:
Prajāpati desired: ‘May I sacrifice with the horse sacrifice.’
He performed ascetic fervor.
From him, as he was heating himself,
seven deities went upward out of his self;
that became the initiation.
He saw these Vaiśvadeva [offerings];
he offered them;
by them he obtained the initiation.
When he offers the Vaiśvadeva [oblations],
by them the sacrificer obtains the initiation.

He offers seven,
for seven were those deities that departed.
He offers day by day;
day by day he secures the initiation.
He offers three Vaiśvadeva [oblations],
four elevation offerings;
they amount to seven.
Seven indeed are the vital breaths in the head;
the vital breaths are the initiation;
by means of the vital breaths he secures the vital breaths, the initiation.

He offers twenty-one Vaiśvadeva [oblations];
twenty-one indeed are the divine realms:
twelve months, five seasons,
these three worlds;
yonder sun is the twenty-first.
This is the heavenly realm;
that is divine regal power;
that is royal majesty;
that is the summit of the sun;
that is called self-sovereignty.

He offers thirty elevation offerings;
the virāj consists of thirty syllables;
the virāj is food;
by means of the virāj he secures proper food.
Dividing it threefold, he offers to the deity.
Threefold indeed are the gods;
threefold are these worlds;
for the attainment of these worlds,
for the proper arrangement of these worlds.
The vital breaths depart from him,

who lets the initiation run over.
They perform [the rite] for seven days;
seven indeed are the vital breaths in the head;
the vital breaths are the initiation;
by means of the vital breaths he secures the vital breaths, the initiation.
He offers a full oblation at the end.
The full oblation is indeed the all;
he attains the all.
Moreover, this [earth] indeed is the full oblation;
he firmly establishes himself on it.

3.8.22.m:
They pen up mares,
for pairing, for procreation;
moreover, that is just as assistant singers accompany the singing.
'The sacrificial horse has chanted,' he says.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati;
Prajāpati is the udgītha;
he secures the udgītha;
moreover, he firmly establishes himself in the ṛc and the sāman.
He presents [it] with gold.
Gold is light;
he places light in the front,
both in the sacrificer and in his offspring;
moreover, with the light of gold the sacrificer goes to the heavenly realm.

3.8.23.m:
Therefore they establish the front fire in the east.
One dedicated to Pūṣan behind [it];
Pūṣan is food;
therefore they bring the food-portion to the front fire.
One dedicated to Indra and Pūṣan above;
the Rājanya is connected with Indra, Pūṣan is food;
with food he grasps him from both sides;
therefore the Rājanya tends to be an eater of food.
Two dedicated to Agni, black-necked, on the two forelegs;
he places heroic power in the two arms;

therefore the Rājanya tends to be strong in the arms.
Two dedicated to Tvaṣṭṛ, with hairy thighs, on the two hind thighs;
he places heroic power in the two thighs;
therefore the Rājanya tends to be strong in the thighs.
Two white-backed ones dedicated to Bṛhaspati on the back;
he places spiritual preeminence on top;
moreover, these two encompass [him] on both sides like two coats of mail;
therefore the Rājanya, clad in armor, performs heroic power.
For Dhātṛ, one with a speckled belly underneath;
he makes this a firm foundation;
moreover, this [earth] is Dhātṛ;
he establishes himself on this [earth].
A white one dedicated to Sūrya at the tail;
he makes that a stronghold;
therefore subjects resort to a stronghold in times of danger.

3.9.2.m:
the sacrificer, having reached the Virāj, obtains her with them.
Eleven decades [of animals] are seized.
The Triṣṭubh has eleven syllables;
domestic animals belong to the Triṣṭubh;
he thus obtains domestic animals.
The horse is sacred to all gods.
The domestic animals have various deities,
for the completeness of the horse.
They are of varied forms;
therefore domestic animals are of varied forms.
They are multi-formed;
therefore domestic animals are multi-formed, for prosperity.

3.9.3.m:
are there many desires in this world?’
Because different animals are seized for the same deities,
he thereby places desires in this world;
therefore in this world there are many desires.
He offers the omentum of three [animals] together.
Threefold are the gods;
threefold are these worlds,
for the attainment of these worlds,
for the proper ordering of these worlds.
They release the wild ones after carrying fire around them, for non-injury.

3.9.4.m:
‘They harness the tawny one,’ he says.
That sun, indeed, is the tawny one;
he harnesses the sun for him.
‘The ruddy one,’ he says.
Agni, indeed, is the ruddy one;
he harnesses Agni for him.
‘The moving one,’ he says.
Vāyu, indeed, is the moving one;
he harnesses Vāyu for him.
‘Standing around,’ he says;

these worlds, indeed, stand around;
he harnesses these worlds for him.
‘The lights shine in heaven,’ he says.
The lunar mansions, indeed, are the lights in heaven;
he causes the lunar mansions to shine for him.
‘They harness his beloved ones,’ he says.
He harnesses his desires for him.
‘The two bay steeds on either side,’ he says.
These two [worlds], indeed, are the two bay steeds on either side;
he harnesses these two for him.

‘Crimson, bold, bearing men,’ he says.
Day and night, indeed, are the two that bear men;
he harnesses day and night for him.
These deities he harnesses for him,
for the attainment of the heavenly realm.
‘Making a banner for that which has no banner,’ he fastens on the flag;
he makes him attain glory among kings.
‘It becomes like the face of a cloud,’ he says;
this is in accordance with the yajus.
‘Those paths of yours, O Savitṛ, which are ancient,’ the Adhvaryu makes the sacrificer recite, for conquering.

With ‘bhūr, bhuvaḥ, svar,’ sacred to Prajāpati, they weave [them] in.
The horse is sacred to Prajāpati;
with its own deity they cause it to thrive.
‘Bhūr’—the chief queen;
‘bhuvaḥ’—the favorite wife;
‘svar’—the discarded wife;
for the conquest of these worlds.
The beads are made of gold.
Gold is light;
the horse sacrifice is sovereignty.

3.9.6.m:
and they fan him;
and they make amends to him.
Three times they go around: three are these worlds;
they fan him from these worlds.
Three times again they go around;
they amount to six;
six are the seasons;
they fan him by means of the seasons.
The vital breaths depart from them

who perform the fanning at the sacrifice.
Nine times they go around.
Nine are the vital breaths in man.
They place the vital breaths in themselves;
the vital breaths do not depart from them.
With "O mother, little mother, dear mother!" he leads forward the wife.
He calls her.
"O fortunate one, dweller in Kāmpīla," he says;
he brings her to austerity.
"Cover yourselves together in the heavenly realm," he says;

this is just as the formula.
There are three kinds of needles:
of iron, of silver, and of gold.
The iron ones are the form of this world;
the silver ones, of the atmosphere;
the gold ones, of the sky.
The iron ones are the cardinal directions;
the silver ones, the intermediate directions;
the gold ones, the zenith.
He arranges the directions for him.
"Who cuts you? Who carves you?" he says, for non-injury.
